  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of neon-19 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=4.5{e}^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in seconds. The half-life of neon-19 is $17.2\\,\\text{s}.$ Rounded to **three significant figures**, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\u2248$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{s}}^{-1}$",
  "worked_solution": "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{\u2032}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=17.2\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n17.2=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}\\,\u21d2\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{17.2}\u22480.0403\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.\n$$",
